    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d876">

                <pb facs="1005" n="893" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg893"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.6363 Manchuria/164: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-04-12T18:00:00-05:00">April 12, 1935—6 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>61. Your 77, April 10, 7 p.m., paragraph 4. British Ambassador on April 10 left
                    at Department a typed plain page<anchor n="86" xml:id="d876-f1"/> bearing no
                    designation, date or signature, which reads in part to the effect that the
                    Anglo-Dutch oil companies have suggested that the California companies, if given
                    a hint by the American Government, would be prepared to unite with the British
                    companies in refusing to provide crude oil supplies for the refinery of the
                    Manchuria Oil Company.</p>
                <p>Department notes apparent discrepancy between foregoing statement and report
                    given in your 77, April 10, 7 p.m., paragraph 4, which refers to supplies of
                    crude oil “to Japan.”</p>
                <p>British Embassy requests expression of Department’s views on whole subject.
                    Department is considering.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
